"Represents category counts with repeated identical symbols, each corresponding to a declared quantity.\n\nUse when: The audience benefits from counting tangible units rather than reading an axis. Counts are small enough for repeated symbols to remain legible.\n\nAvoid when: Precise comparisons involve many fractional symbols or a huge quantity range.\n\nVisual grammar: The number of equal-sized symbols encodes quantity. Rows identify categories; a key declares the quantity represented by one symbol.\n\nBuild: Choose a symbol unit that limits repetition while preserving useful precision. Repeat identical symbols on a regular grid within each category row. Label exact values and explain any partial symbol convention.\n\nCheck: Verify every complete symbol represents the same quantity. Check partial symbols correspond to the stated fractional counts. Confirm spacing and symbol size remain constant across rows.\n\nPitfalls: Scaling icon area while also repeating icons double-encodes magnitude incorrectly. Avoid detailed icons whose visible area differs even with identical bounding boxes.\n\nExample: Show synthetic community book donations with one identical book symbol per 20 books and exact row totals.\n\nAlternatives: Bar chart, Waffle chart, Wilkinson dot plot."
